  "type": "multipleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "If the pressure, temperature and density of an ideal gas are denoted by $p, T$ and $\\rho$, respectively, the velocity of sound in the gas is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "DtRBZ",
      "text": "proportional to $\\sqrt{p},$ when $T$ is constant.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "K4XYz",
      "text": "proportional to $\\sqrt{T}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "dM8Fa",
      "text": "proportional to $\\sqrt{p}$, when $\\rho$ is constant.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "G9PJL",
      "text": "proportional to $T$.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$v=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma R T}{M}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma P}{\\rho}}$\r\n(i) Velocity of sound is proportional to $\\sqrt{T}$\r\n$\\therefore$\r\n$v \\propto \\sqrt{T}$\r\n(ii) When $\\rho$ is constant, the velocity of sound is proportional to root of pressure $P$\r\n$\\therefore$\r\n$$\r\nv \\propto \\sqrt{P}\r\n$$",
    "image": null
